<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.wland.plist

  Boot-time launchd plist for wland, the NextBSD 802.11 management daemon.

  WHY org.nextbsd.* AND NOT com.apple.*

  Every NextBSD launchd job is labelled org.nextbsd.* (nextbsd-userland#437),
  including the daemons ported from Apple (org.nextbsd.configd,
  org.nextbsd.notifyd, org.nextbsd.IPConfiguration). Those daemons still check
  in Apple's Mach service names, because Apple-derived clients look the
  services up by exact string (a clean-room Security.framework carries
  bootstrap_look_up("com.apple.securityd") in its own source).

  Neither reason for an Apple service name applies here. Apple's daemon is
  `wifid`; ours is `wland`, so com.apple.wland would be a FABRICATED Apple
  name for a daemon Apple never shipped. And nothing Apple-derived looks this
  service up: we write the `wlan` CLI, we write the System Preferences backend,
  and the CoreWLAN-shaped ObjC API lives above the Mach line in Gershwin. Both
  ends are ours, so the service name (org.nextbsd.wlan) is ours too.

  WHAT WLAND DOES

  1. Clones the 802.11 VAP off each radio (`ifconfig wlan0 create wlandev
     iwlwifi0` — on stock FreeBSD that is rc.conf's job, and NextBSD has no
     rc.conf and no /etc/rc.d, so it had no owner at all).
  2. Spawns and drives STOCK wpa_supplicant from base over its control socket,
     with an EMPTY config, so wland is the sole author of every network block.
  3. Publishes State:/Network/Interface/<if>/AirPort {Authenticated, SSID,
     BSSID, RSSI, Channel, Security} — which is the predicate ipconfigd's DHCP
     gate waits on. Without this key, DHCP on WLAN does not work AT ALL: net80211
     reports the link UP at association, before the WPA 4-way handshake, so
     ipconfigd would DHCP onto an unauthenticated port and every packet would be
     dropped.

  RunAtLoad + KeepAlive: starts at PID-1 launchd boot and respawns on crash.
  There is NO launchd service ordering in this port (no StartAfter, no Requires,
  and the plist directory scan is not even sorted), so wland must NOT assume
  configd is up when it starts — it opens the SCDynamicStore lazily and tolerates
  NULL, which is the same pattern ipconfigd's sc_publish.c uses.

  StandardErrorPath / StandardOutPath route the log to /var/log/wland.stderr,
  matching the ipconfigd / configd convention; the CI boot test dumps these
  post-boot.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.wland</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/sbin/wland</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
    <key>MachServices</key>
    <dict>
        <key>org.nextbsd.wlan</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/wland.stderr</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/wland.stderr</string>
</dict>
</plist>
